1
00:00:01,750 --> 00:00:03,186
Your music just stopped.

2
00:00:03,206 --> 00:00:06,145
So how did your AirPods
know you took one out?

3
00:00:06,550 --> 00:00:11,425
Inside, there's a tiny skin-detect sensor,
right where the bud touches your ear.

4
00:00:11,445 --> 00:00:14,593
An infrared light, with
a light sensor beside it.

5
00:00:14,990 --> 00:00:18,962
When it's in your ear, it shines
invisible light into your skin,

6
00:00:18,982 --> 00:00:20,708
and reads what bounces back.

7
00:00:20,728 --> 00:00:23,311
Your skin sends it back in its own way.

8
00:00:23,710 --> 00:00:26,879
A table, or a pocket,
doesn't match that pattern.

9
00:00:26,899 --> 00:00:30,784
Older AirPods used simpler
sensors that a pocket could fool.

10
00:00:30,804 --> 00:00:32,841
This one can tell skin apart.

11
00:00:33,240 --> 00:00:36,405
The moment it stops seeing
skin, the H2 chip knows,

12
00:00:36,425 --> 00:00:39,385
and tells your phone
over Bluetooth to pause.

13
00:00:39,680 --> 00:00:43,307
Put it back in, it sees skin
again, and your music plays on.

14
00:00:44,330 --> 00:00:46,622
Follow InvisiTech for more.
